{"t":"msg","chat":"m2resume","parts":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Maintain a consistent sleep schedule by going to bed and waking up at the same time every day, including weekends, to reinforce your body's natural circadian rhythm.\n\n2. Create a calming pre-bedtime routine that signals to your brain it is time to wind down, such as reading a book, taking a warm bath, or practicing gentle stretching.\n\n3. Optimize your bedroom environment by keeping it cool, dark, and quiet, and invest in a comfortable mattress and supportive pillows suited to your preferred sleeping position.\n\n4. Limit exposure to blue light from phones, tablets, computers, and televisions for at least one hour before bed, since it suppresses melatonin production and delays sleep onset.\n\n5. Avoid caffeine, nicotine, and other stimulants in the late afternoon and evening because they can linger in your system for hours and disrupt your ability to fall asleep.\n\n6. Engage in regular physical activity during the day, but finish vigorous exercise at least three hours before bedtime so your body has adequate time to cool down and relax.\n\n7. Be mindful of your evening meals by avoiding heavy, spicy, or very large dinners close to bedtime, and limit excessive fluid intake to prevent nighttime awakenings.\n\n8. If you cannot fall asleep after about twenty minutes, get out of bed and do a quiet, low-light activity until you feel sleepy, then return to bed to reinforce the association between bed and sleep.\n\n9. Manage stress and racing thoughts through techniques such as deep breathing, meditation, or journaling before bed, since mental arousal is one of the most common barriers to restful sleep.\n\n10. Reserve your bed primarily for sleep and intimacy, avoiding work, eating, or prolonged wakefulness in bed, so your brain learns to associate it with rest rather than activity."}],"seq":45}
